MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_001: Ahhh. I'm spent.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_002: Hey, hey... It's only noon, boss! What's with all\nthe yawning?
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_003: I was up late, thinking—thinking about how to get\nto the kingdom of the sea beasts on the ocean floor...
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_004: What? You just woke up from a three-year nap!\nYou're telling me you're already sleep-deprived?
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_005: Nothing I can do about it, is there? Besides, there's a\ndifference between normal sleep and vacation sleep.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_006: When I'm on vacation, I'm gone, like a candle that's\nbeen blown out. It's not exactly restful sleep, you see.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_007: Must be nice. While you were out there being a\ncandle, some of us were out there working.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_008: I've been meaning to ask... Anything interesting\nhappen while I was away?
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_009: Let's see... There was this one thing...
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_010: I took this job protecting a village from marauders,\nand I happened to have that blade you gave me.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_011: Well, one of the marauders broke my weapon,\nbut I drew the blade fast enough to counter.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_012: I got a hit in, lost my grip, and he took the chance to\nrun off with the damn thing stuck in his arm.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_013: Hahaha! Of course he did! That's how it always\ngoes, isn't it?
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_014: That "blade" was a ritual dagger, you know.\nI gave it to you for its value, not its deadliness!
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_015: Makes sense—now. I thought it was weird the thing\nbarely had an edge on it.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_016: The guy probably ran off with it because he knew\nit was worth some money.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_017: Heh. You may be good in a fight, but your eye for\ntreasure could use some work, Simon.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_018: Eh. Everything turned out well enough. Spent a night\nwith one of the village women when it was over.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_019: Well, then the tale has a happy ending! So what\nhappened to her?
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_020: It only lasted as long as the job. Once it was over,\nI left, obviously.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_021: No attachments, huh? Sounds like the Simon\nI know, alright! Ahahaha!
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_022: Incredible stuff... If you remember any other stories\nlike that, you come find me.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_C_023: I've got as many stories as you could want.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_001: Hey, Simon. Out of curiosity, how long have you\nbeen the way you are?
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_002: What do you mean by that? How long have I been\na bastard?
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_003: Hmhmhm. That can wait. I meant how long have\nyou been tempting death as a mercenary?
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_004: Ah, gotcha. The questions aren't as different as you\nmight think, actually.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_005: I was born in a village in the Castalia region,\nsmack in the middle of Laverna.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_006: I learned to use a sword young, expected to help\ndefend the village whenever raiders came.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_007: Hm. Things must've been rough if they were relying\non kids for protection.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_008: Yeah, I guess. But I loved every minute of it.\nSure, all I knew was fighting, but I was happy.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_009: I outgrew my teacher by the time I turned 18 and\nstarted crowing about how strong I was.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_010: Then, one day, when I was away, those bandits\nshowed up and burned the village down.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_011: Everyone died. Everyone I knew. My teacher,\nmy childhood sweetheart I was engaged to marry...
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_012: I tracked the bandits down. I killed them all—wiped\nthem out. But not without nearly dying myself.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_013: I took all my rage out on them. Or I tried to. But even\nseeing them dead didn't put out the fire inside me.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_014: Living with a sword in hand, with the ability and\nreason to use it... That's the feeling I kept chasing.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_015: And so you chose to live as a mercenary.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_016: I found myself in a lawless region, so it wasn't hard\nto find work, and I made a name for myself.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_017: I saved up and even settled down for a bit, but I gave\nup the girl and our life together to go back to war.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_018: On the field, looking death in the eye, is the only\nplace I feel alive—the only place I can be...me.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_019: So I kept at it. Then one day, I got a job doing guard\nduty for Vandahl Trading.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_020: And that's how we met. I have a good idea of how\nthe story goes from there.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_021: Well, there you have it. The story of a mercenary\nbastard's creation.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_022: Don't say it like that. It's a good story. It helps me\nunderstand what drives you.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_023: You've got a strange taste in stories, boss. Look at\nyou, grinning away.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_024: What can I say. I like hearing about where a person's\ndreams and desires come from.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_025: I'm a merchant. I want to help see those desires\nfulfilled, so I like to hear how they started too.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_026: Thank you for telling me your story, Simon. I'm glad\nwe have you on board.
MID_SE_Tal_Sim_B_027: Hehe. Spare me the humble act. We both know\nI'm no match for you.
